WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.140 --> 00:00:02.340
- This isn't the story I expected to tell.

00:00:02.340 --> 00:00:04.360
I knew, coming here, that this
isn't some magic beach

00:00:04.360 --> 00:00:07.160
where you can bring a shovel and
scoop up buckets and buckets of Lego.

00:00:07.160 --> 00:00:08.950
It might've felt a bit like that
twenty years ago,

00:00:08.950 --> 00:00:11.360
but these days Lego is
a little harder to find.

00:00:11.360 --> 00:00:14.340
But yes, here on the coast of Cornwall,
in the south-west of England,

00:00:14.340 --> 00:00:15.960
when the conditions are right,

00:00:15.960 --> 00:00:20.340
you might find quite a bit of Lego
scattered on Perranporth beach.

00:00:20.340 --> 00:00:22.910
In 1997, a container ship
called Tokio Express

00:00:22.910 --> 00:00:25.215
was on its way to New York
when it got hit by a heavy storm 

00:00:25.215 --> 00:00:26.810
off Land's End,
just west of here.

00:00:26.810 --> 00:00:29.160
A load of containers got
knocked into the sea.

00:00:29.160 --> 00:00:33.640
And one of them contained almost 5 million
little plastic pieces of Lego.

00:00:33.640 --> 00:00:35.070
Most of which were ocean themed.

00:00:35.070 --> 00:00:36.450
Flippers, spears, octopuses,

00:00:36.450 --> 00:00:38.550
all crashing around in
the waves and currents.

00:00:38.550 --> 00:00:40.750
The far end of this beach
has the right conditions.

00:00:40.750 --> 00:00:42.060
There's a huge expanse of sand,

00:00:42.060 --> 00:00:43.820
the wind is in the right direction,

00:00:43.820 --> 00:00:46.470
but the pieces can wash up anywhere.

00:00:46.470 --> 00:00:49.290
That's the nice, cute story
that I was going to tell.

00:00:49.290 --> 00:00:52.840
- We find the Lego after high spring tides
and winter storms.

00:00:52.840 --> 00:00:55.510
And that's when the waves
will eat into the dunes

00:00:55.510 --> 00:01:00.380
and release a lot of the plastic that's
been washed ashore many years ago.

00:01:00.380 --> 00:01:01.300
- That's Tracey Williams.

00:01:01.300 --> 00:01:02.840
She's been beachcombing here for years

00:01:02.840 --> 00:01:05.550
and runs a photography project
called Lego Lost at Sea.

00:01:05.550 --> 00:01:06.880
Tracey didn't want to be on camera,

00:01:06.880 --> 00:01:08.330
which is frankly sensible.

00:01:08.330 --> 00:01:11.180
But she was nice enough to show off
some of her Lego collection,

00:01:11.180 --> 00:01:13.850
to take me beachcombing here
on the far end of the beach,

00:01:13.850 --> 00:01:15.970
and to chat about what
she's found over the years.

00:01:15.970 --> 00:01:18.950
So I got my GoPro ready.
We went out for a walk on the shore.

00:01:18.950 --> 00:01:21.830
And that's where the story I
thought I was going to tell

00:01:21.830 --> 00:01:23.480
didn't work anymore.

00:01:23.480 --> 00:01:25.069
When you've got it in a little jar

00:01:25.069 --> 00:01:25.902
[Tracy laughs]

00:01:25.902 --> 00:01:28.788
with all the bright colours,
it looks almost quaint.

00:01:28.788 --> 00:01:29.740
And then the reality is a bit different.

00:01:29.740 --> 00:01:32.670
- No, the reality is horrifying, isn't it?

00:01:32.670 --> 00:01:34.893
So if you look here, see.

00:01:36.140 --> 00:01:37.000
- Absolutely full.

00:01:37.000 --> 00:01:39.370
- So this is actually a plastic pebble.

00:01:39.370 --> 00:01:41.036
- Plastic?
- Yeah.

00:01:41.036 --> 00:01:42.713
- How can you tell the difference?
- Feel the weight.

00:01:43.610 --> 00:01:44.513
- Oh, huh!

00:01:45.410 --> 00:01:46.840
- Nobody really knows
where they're coming from,

00:01:46.840 --> 00:01:49.310
but they're thought to be the
remains of beach bonfires.

00:01:49.310 --> 00:01:52.290
So I've actually picked
up 20,000 of those.

00:01:52.290 --> 00:01:54.237
It's actually from an ale cask.

00:01:54.237 --> 00:01:56.720
These are from packaging strips.

00:01:56.720 --> 00:02:00.570
These are what they put round cartons
to protect them from packaging.

00:02:00.570 --> 00:02:04.880
Shotgun wads, shotgun cartridges.
Bottle caps.

00:02:04.880 --> 00:02:06.020
- I mean, what do you do with...

00:02:06.020 --> 00:02:08.520
like, you've just got to bring a
trash bag and just...

00:02:08.520 --> 00:02:11.150
- Yeah, so I normally come
down armed with sacks.

00:02:11.150 --> 00:02:12.990
That's a glow stick from fishing.

00:02:12.990 --> 00:02:16.230
So that's the valve off a tyre.
- Oh yeah!

00:02:16.230 --> 00:02:20.040
- So these get discarded on
garage forecourts and tyre places,

00:02:20.040 --> 00:02:23.000
and they just get washed into the drains.
- And the drains go straight to the sea.

00:02:23.000 --> 00:02:25.030
- A lot will be litter dropped
on the streets.

00:02:25.030 --> 00:02:27.800
80% of litter dropped on the streets

00:02:27.800 --> 00:02:30.976
is said to make its way to the sea.
- Wow.

00:02:30.976 --> 00:02:33.370
It's not just Lego,
that's the way people like to tell it

00:02:33.370 --> 00:02:34.580
but it's not just Lego.

00:02:34.580 --> 00:02:39.400
It's so many plastic things
and it's not just this beach.

00:02:39.400 --> 00:02:42.300
Once you start looking...
immediately, bright white,

00:02:42.300 --> 00:02:44.563
bright blue, bright yellow.
- Yeah.

00:02:44.563 --> 00:02:45.530
- If I was looking at
this from a distance,

00:02:45.530 --> 00:02:47.720
I would have just read it all as seaweed.

00:02:47.720 --> 00:02:50.133
- It's actually not as bad today,

00:02:50.970 --> 00:02:52.020
as it sometimes is,

00:02:52.020 --> 00:02:54.280
but I can't see how it's
ever going to get better

00:02:54.280 --> 00:02:56.250
in a way it's a sort
of pointless exercise,

00:02:56.250 --> 00:02:58.070
constantly picking it up.

00:02:58.070 --> 00:03:01.310
- I've just got a sort-of
creeping horror at this point.

00:03:01.310 --> 00:03:02.143
Do you get used to that?

00:03:02.143 --> 00:03:04.520
- Get used to seeing
this amount of plastic?

00:03:04.520 --> 00:03:06.320
- Yeah.
- Yeah.

00:03:06.320 --> 00:03:08.070
- There's this cliché in horror movies,

00:03:08.070 --> 00:03:10.650
where the characters suddenly realise
that the monster that they've heard about,

00:03:10.650 --> 00:03:12.760
this abstract thing, is actually

00:03:12.760 --> 00:03:15.220
in the room with them and has
been there the whole time,

00:03:15.220 --> 00:03:17.760
and that's kind of what this felt like,

00:03:17.760 --> 00:03:19.860
because I've been going to
beaches like this all my life

00:03:19.860 --> 00:03:24.010
and I'd never noticed the bright colours
that were right in front of me.

00:03:24.010 --> 00:03:27.600
- So on this beach,
you get a lot of the floating plastic.

00:03:27.600 --> 00:03:29.977
So you've got all the
fragments of plastic.

00:03:29.977 --> 00:03:32.940
You got the nurdles,
these little tiny nurdles.

00:03:32.940 --> 00:03:33.863
- Those aren't stones? Those are...

00:03:33.863 --> 00:03:36.740
- No, these are little plastic nurdles.

00:03:36.740 --> 00:03:40.780
These are the raw material
that all plastic items are made from.

00:03:40.780 --> 00:03:42.020
There's billions of them.

00:03:42.020 --> 00:03:43.930
So some are nurdles.

00:03:43.930 --> 00:03:47.420
You also get ones that
are black and ridged

00:03:47.420 --> 00:03:49.720
and they're actually
biobeads that are used

00:03:49.720 --> 00:03:51.990
in waste-water treatment plants.

00:03:51.990 --> 00:03:56.046
- And then you sort-of pull back the camera
and you realise that it's all of this.

00:03:56.046 --> 00:03:57.430
There's so much of it.

00:03:57.430 --> 00:04:01.940
- On some beaches, you get the plastic
that sinks to the ocean floor

00:04:01.940 --> 00:04:05.180
and it washes ashore with the kelp,
with the brown seaweed.

00:04:05.180 --> 00:04:07.530
They've probably been
swirling around the seabed

00:04:07.530 --> 00:04:10.030
for the last 24 years,

00:04:10.030 --> 00:04:12.410
and every now and again they
wash ashore with the seaweed.

00:04:12.410 --> 00:04:13.957
- And this goes down, then?

00:04:13.957 --> 00:04:15.610
- We assume it does.

00:04:15.610 --> 00:04:18.896
- So a lot of it is is buried under where
we are now. Just needs to come out.

00:04:18.896 --> 00:04:22.640
- Quite a lot of the toys we find,
you can date them.

00:04:22.640 --> 00:04:25.660
And some date back to the 50s and 60s.

00:04:25.660 --> 00:04:27.960
Especially the cereal packet toys.

00:04:27.960 --> 00:04:30.100
We recently did some
experiments in the lab

00:04:30.100 --> 00:04:34.540
to see how long Lego would last
in the marine or coastal environments,

00:04:34.540 --> 00:04:37.100
and the scientists that did
the experiments worked out

00:04:37.100 --> 00:04:39.810
that it could last up to 1300 years.

00:04:39.810 --> 00:04:43.801
I wonder if one day you'll just be able
to recognise it by the colour.

00:04:43.801 --> 00:04:44.634
- Yeah.

00:04:44.634 --> 00:04:47.430
- Bright green of the seaweed
or the yellow of the lifeboats.

00:04:47.430 --> 00:04:48.930
Ah, look, treasure!

00:04:48.930 --> 00:04:52.350
- Treasure? Is that one of
the dwarves from Snow White?

00:04:52.350 --> 00:04:54.370
It's not every beach,
but it's a lot of them.

00:04:54.370 --> 00:04:56.340
Tucked away in corners
where the tourists don't go

00:04:56.340 --> 00:04:57.770
or just in plain sight.

00:04:57.770 --> 00:05:00.360
There was seaweed on another
beach filled with plastic.

00:05:00.360 --> 00:05:01.980
Once you see it...!

00:05:01.980 --> 00:05:03.540
- I've just seen a Lego broom.

00:05:03.540 --> 00:05:04.900
- Yes. Just there?

00:05:04.900 --> 00:05:06.470
- Yes.

00:05:06.470 --> 00:05:08.800
- What do you know? Thank you.

00:05:08.800 --> 00:05:13.730
Among all the other plastic waste,
there still is Lego washing up.

00:05:13.730 --> 00:05:16.500
Did they used to be more Lego?
Has it changed over the years?

00:05:16.500 --> 00:05:19.183
- Yes. I think there's definitely less
Lego than there used to be.

00:05:19.183 --> 00:05:21.470
And there's lots of people
looking out for it now.

00:05:21.470 --> 00:05:24.090
You only ever see what floats really.

00:05:24.090 --> 00:05:27.200
You never really hear about
all the items from cargo spills

00:05:27.200 --> 00:05:28.730
that sink to the bottom of the sea.

00:05:28.730 --> 00:05:31.460
- Because the rest of it is going to be out
on the ocean floor somewhere.

00:05:31.460 --> 00:05:33.126
- Yeah.
- That's bleak.

00:05:33.126 --> 00:05:34.790
[both laugh]

00:05:34.790 --> 00:05:36.270
I don't have a neat ending for the story.

00:05:36.270 --> 00:05:38.593
I was going to say, "Oh, if you're
on the beach in south-west England,

00:05:38.593 --> 00:05:40.360
!it's worth keeping your
eyes down from time to time.

00:05:40.360 --> 00:05:42.270
"Maybe you'll see some Lego!"

00:05:42.270 --> 00:05:44.100
But even if you don't,

00:05:44.100 --> 00:05:47.470
maybe take a bag with you
and take some plastic away,

00:05:47.470 --> 00:05:48.820
because there's a lot of it.

00:05:50.090 --> 00:05:52.810
The Lego Lost at Sea project is on
Instagram, Twitter, and Facebook,

00:05:52.810 --> 00:05:54.460
and there's also a link
to pre-order Tracey's book

00:05:54.460 --> 00:05:55.660
down in the description.

00:05:56.531 --> 00:05:57.720
[chuckles]

00:05:57.720 --> 00:06:00.310
If I looked slightly distracted during that

00:06:00.310 --> 00:06:03.180
it's because halfway through
I realised that there is

00:06:06.120 --> 00:06:08.210
green nylon rope just in the seaweed,

00:06:08.210 --> 00:06:09.413
just hanging out there.

